#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ce6ab5-0c7a-51c2-a7c1-3dde93bf13aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327115} {:answer/id #uuid "18d002b6-3c00-5324-a52f-d2c2cc81367e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327150} {:answer/id #uuid "267b631f-8cd5-56ca-8970-47e5d3e71a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327269} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb36713-2be9-5718-9a33-aca0c855d118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327617} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c8b6d1-816f-510d-8fe5-d364e7879e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c8b6d1-816f-510d-8fe5-d364e7879e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328644} :answer-field/id #uuid "2008970d-386c-52f0-bfa3-0b9597977dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "182c44b5-94da-5e7b-aa87-a9b6286e7135" :question/math-academy-id "q-47745" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}?$\n\n- Its domain is $x>0$\n- Its range is $-\\infty <y<\\infty $\n- It has a horizontal asymptote $y=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=(0.125)^{x}:$\n\n![](images/20/20fae3a813e249a6423567b448a1f5515c3b9127e7c0b82aad541e720db03aca.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)=(0.125)^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $y=0$ is a horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1504c4b0-2502-5021-a3b2-5fdfff9d60ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327117} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2c6c96-8d84-5172-bdab-f5fb81de282d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327300} {:answer/id #uuid "8337c1a8-990e-5af4-82fd-847210c287b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328066} {:answer/id #uuid "bb449a61-7ad1-571d-9098-6a0609fcfb09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328568} {:answer/id #uuid "e1be7aa5-9f5e-5bd0-8d21-aae473847230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1504c4b0-2502-5021-a3b2-5fdfff9d60ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327117} :answer-field/id #uuid "161d2ec7-038b-5b21-b964-a5c78124ff2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f25b7dd1-f51b-52ea-a0c4-08f9ed823c0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47747" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}?$\n\n- Its domain is $-\\infty <x<\\infty $\n- It's a decreasing function\n- $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/28/288adf3d1da5cf4bb6e2530318aeb083b69d43639de7704f9df15672be477d0a.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c3d09b-9cb8-5c35-b434-fbf3a754bd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327712} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab548fd-70c3-51ff-a970-7842ff9d3073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327727} {:answer/id #uuid "7f237cd6-cf90-5d76-937c-5b0d021dcf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328026} {:answer/id #uuid "940d7e4b-771a-5d4f-a09e-e2cf491e260b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328226} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8b12b2-dbc1-5c8a-8acc-f14956735d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f237cd6-cf90-5d76-937c-5b0d021dcf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328026} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e4da35-1cc4-5532-8339-deb43a0d0d5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "764f6881-905e-5e2e-b6a1-fb2e4b876502" :question/math-academy-id "q-48271" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $g(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-6,\\, & x<1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $g(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $g(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $g(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)-6 \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}g(x) & ={1}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =g(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023faeb9-4a15-5b83-b97b-616d63fcb5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329983} {:answer/id #uuid "680bf84b-55c8-5631-988f-84b2bfa4aff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330886} {:answer/id #uuid "971b2be8-f600-57bb-b5fb-1198580f3fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331293} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b45324-38b6-5584-a18b-8e58f431248b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331848} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f0becd-2719-5504-8aef-9fe7923357f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680bf84b-55c8-5631-988f-84b2bfa4aff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330886} :answer-field/id #uuid "de379682-e9de-5683-a723-9cc521e3f6b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31bc62dc-b78f-5686-a2a7-468fc236acf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-49026" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=3$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x=0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{3}}{{e}^{-1}}=4e>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{3(4)^{2}-(4)^{3}}{{e}^{4}}=-\\frac{16}{{e}^{4}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points at Which a Function is Not Differentiable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e68ed1-6c21-5339-b817-0dca6bed6e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330010} {:answer/id #uuid "13dd78f4-7836-5e2c-95cf-18fff06f843d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330114} {:answer/id #uuid "36a4f226-546a-58fe-9e0a-5f6172d50d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330439} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbffc66-920f-5159-966d-348e80294b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331207} {:answer/id #uuid "fea5d391-e5d1-5006-b2d7-39671ae5c2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fea5d391-e5d1-5006-b2d7-39671ae5c2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332194} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ab9f03-8ca5-52dc-a6f7-f2d81ee5a0b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "227ca0fe-b4ce-570a-82ea-2de7f8f95c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-51067" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$, has a cusp at $x=1,$ and is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\nI. It is differentiable at $x=-2$\n\nII. It's **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x+2|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a cusp at $x=1$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115381 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Functions are Differentiable at a Given Point From the Function Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4edaf09a-43ea-57b9-acbf-f37078f4f41c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330665} {:answer/id #uuid "940c2245-8eff-5f28-9ff9-b6f020a49afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331263} {:answer/id #uuid "de0ae6a9-dc8c-5d6d-81ca-a6a089d7492b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331913} {:answer/id #uuid "ed63afa6-ed56-546d-b92e-5cd7efd76af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332036} {:answer/id #uuid "fe84f7ce-c9f5-5fd2-a3fe-29ac648392b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "940c2245-8eff-5f28-9ff9-b6f020a49afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331263} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcf8469a-015f-5427-bd41-e940a4f01f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce07619-7707-5d93-86b3-865c0def2d71" :question/math-academy-id "q-51072" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ x+2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nI. It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n\nII. It is differentiable on the interval $(-2,2)$\n\nIII. It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false and statement III is true. Indeed,  let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-x+4)=-1+4=3 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+2)=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it has a removable discontinuity there). Hence, it is also not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a0b5715-2f16-5b2b-bde0-2f691ab0ca3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330321} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8e62be-06ee-56b6-b837-a4e9dea4318a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330637} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4a5523-4900-5f78-b66a-19becc23b13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330662} {:answer/id #uuid "9d89c5eb-0a45-56c5-8de3-da7e9b6563be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331362} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e018a5-bacd-5ac3-a680-6c33d1bce60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e4a5523-4900-5f78-b66a-19becc23b13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330662} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8ea73e9-6798-5054-beae-dfdff79ed690" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12650667-2b8f-5503-bd94-74dd3c8919fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-51690" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $3{x}^{3}-P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}+P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $3$\n- $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P=3{x}^{3}-1.$ Then $3{x}^{3}-P(x)=3{x}^{3}-(3{x}^{3}-1)=1,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose $P=-3{x}^{3}.$ Then $3{x}^{3}+P(x)=3{x}^{3}+(-3{x}^{3})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{3}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{3}={x}^{3+3}={x}^{6},$ meaning that $3{x}^{3}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $6.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a913ab2-f965-5820-9ac4-825b7f4c1af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333087} {:answer/id #uuid "8a057cf3-36bf-5aa8-b647-3cf1d42597c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334220} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cb08e9-6689-53c0-9c13-46fecb404e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334442} {:answer/id #uuid "a974827c-2dd8-5221-a573-4c25ac3faacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334475} {:answer/id #uuid "d56950a8-cc61-5a5f-92cc-30c0e56e5c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a974827c-2dd8-5221-a573-4c25ac3faacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334475} :answer-field/id #uuid "47bc468c-6252-5415-aa33-0f102ce301b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "260eecb9-d0fb-5697-b47b-f5982b239650" :question/math-academy-id "q-64258" :question/problem "A particle of mass $32\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{8}{32}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\frac{1}{2}$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{2}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f724acc-58aa-50a8-91a2-5350fa28c1c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333126} {:answer/id #uuid "61c8b9df-83d3-5f20-8b97-46c0df53d735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333878} {:answer/id #uuid "82f1dbf2-b0b8-5166-840a-fbb44cfe06f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334158} {:answer/id #uuid "cd26c381-8292-572f-8fe0-b2d2e820f7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334780} {:answer/id #uuid "d38fb8e7-94f0-5bc1-b1b2-08ad0bc92192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd26c381-8292-572f-8fe0-b2d2e820f7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334780} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bf5d69-17b7-5d69-9708-5cb73a5a88ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0dca080f-586e-56e2-9297-7dcd4b704b21" :question/math-academy-id "q-64615" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =-16<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "120a7cf6-5730-5b28-b0d8-c1e791963e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333150} {:answer/id #uuid "1b63744f-e7f3-5e9f-9ee0-eeb45a9ec5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333238} {:answer/id #uuid "3d11d708-2d68-5095-adaa-5073f6069713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333564} {:answer/id #uuid "495a738b-9682-5553-9eb1-4c600690075b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333665} {:answer/id #uuid "97528c2d-847c-599f-9b47-dcdfbe437a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d11d708-2d68-5095-adaa-5073f6069713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333564} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab33f5c8-2a16-511d-b5e0-f528bae70ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c1c35ed-7049-53ec-934c-7b92d30ad741" :question/math-academy-id "q-64616" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(6) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =8>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3df3dfbb-59b2-5929-bc22-0173acdfe0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333574} {:answer/id #uuid "3f42cac2-5418-5976-9b69-0bae1fc98cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333587} {:answer/id #uuid "4967801e-fc06-5c70-90c2-be13df72ee91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333666} {:answer/id #uuid "89033697-d2bd-5567-b5e8-a764a33c2fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334207} {:answer/id #uuid "90d31c44-194e-5317-988d-2587b82e4020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f42cac2-5418-5976-9b69-0bae1fc98cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333587} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6c0dfb4-37f6-502a-87f7-d413f94017ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccd0f4b9-0dbd-51e2-a95c-14471c65ba52" :question/math-academy-id "q-65145" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$\n\nIII. The limit exists and is equal to $0$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124466 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d090462-a031-5f96-ac13-90dac882edab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points Where Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d023b1a-e54c-5a09-986e-c64e8ea0ce13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333981} {:answer/id #uuid "b6aad436-bdd3-559d-8993-f9ec2e12126f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334588} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e883eb-0fef-5f4c-9a6c-403643063014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334661} {:answer/id #uuid "d284e0b7-af0b-5d1a-99e5-eded90f88db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334828} {:answer/id #uuid "f6908825-368b-5836-af84-e8c9e08903f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d284e0b7-af0b-5d1a-99e5-eded90f88db0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334828} :answer-field/id #uuid "c726d8d6-8230-5663-b6a4-988732e72c43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b22bd39f-49d5-56c7-8797-705e83876e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-65255" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true about the function $f(x,y)=xy{e}^{x/y}?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(0,1)$is defined\n\nII. ${f}_{y}(1,0)$ is defined\n\nIII. Both partial derivatives exist at the point $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =y{e}^{x/y}+x{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}+xy{e}^{x/y}(-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x/y}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ itself is defined at $(0,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,1)=0\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=0,\n$$\n\nand ${f}_{x}$ is also defined at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1)=1\\cdot {e}^{0/1}+0\\cdot {e}^{0/1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into ${f}_{y}$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(1,0)=1\\cdot {e}^{1/0}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{0}\\cdot {e}^{1/0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(1,1)=1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}+1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=2e \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,1) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1/1}-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{1}\\cdot {e}^{1/1}=e-e=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176051 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18844536-bb9a-5495-b8f5-d5d13d54642d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333209} {:answer/id #uuid "24bf17c6-2954-5c78-a311-d5deae2b8a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333331} {:answer/id #uuid "8b851376-f804-50a7-b362-23223a125b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334235} {:answer/id #uuid "bec0dfb8-da35-5e82-96fa-936c4dc06a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334641} {:answer/id #uuid "f7cd2af5-0c8c-5c42-96c8-3b4163c4160a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24bf17c6-2954-5c78-a311-d5deae2b8a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333331} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f8778e-5ef0-5013-9066-f769f9eb4a80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e763b192-f670-5ee9-881f-7ff2a2620e1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-65332" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec⁡x?$\n\n- $x=-\\pi $ is not in the domain of the function. $$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is in the range of the function. $$\n- $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ thus it is defined for $x=-\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is not in the range.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $, thus $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338747 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074a7388-71ec-5e98-881d-74c9332197cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336087} {:answer/id #uuid "169710d9-fa73-5e2c-b153-5b77d210f718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336244} {:answer/id #uuid "210e37eb-490e-51ab-a536-e5c68eb69442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336340} {:answer/id #uuid "534e28b2-7d11-5fe1-ac0a-de23e2096d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336800} {:answer/id #uuid "702aacf4-efa4-5ebb-bd51-52a52761993b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "702aacf4-efa4-5ebb-bd51-52a52761993b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337027} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb68fa6d-6c03-55ab-b749-7edacc1c4852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "029d7f47-3f9c-597a-ac75-8fbf40dc3193" :question/math-academy-id "q-70105" :question/problem "Given that $det({A}^{T})=8$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=0$\n- $2\\text{det}(A)+\\text{det}(I)=16$\n- $3\\text{det}(I)-2\\text{det}(A)=-13$" :question/worked-solution "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)=\\text{det}({A}^{T})=8\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-\\text{det}({A}^{T})=8-8=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n2\\text{det}(A)+\\text{det}(I)=2\\cdot 8+1=17\\ne 16.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3\\text{det}(I)-2\\text{det}(A)=3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 8=-13.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129086 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9110c50a-62db-5447-b4d0-60819c80eb31" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties Related to Point Discontinuities Given a Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c47c8e-fbb3-5fee-a481-c31c5f6bdbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336347} {:answer/id #uuid "2a32bc8f-d6fa-5aec-8660-034aacadd789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336426} {:answer/id #uuid "2dafd27c-87f8-54b8-bbdb-622b68b98c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336470} {:answer/id #uuid "7d976a02-f955-5025-a0e1-aca0db00413b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337166} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1ce169-1070-5ff7-9c77-be607c3dad04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dafd27c-87f8-54b8-bbdb-622b68b98c52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336470} :answer-field/id #uuid "91a2900d-cee1-5962-9a23-3192a7cb0a13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b1ad50f-eeb7-5cca-b60f-fb6418344feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-71180" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}4x, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(2)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}(4x)=8, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}{x}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=2$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a64777c-56a5-5219-9978-577e6ce97a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336127} {:answer/id #uuid "924d685b-06d1-54a6-83d2-4d89ca9bcafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337360} {:answer/id #uuid "99e11deb-a3d7-5bb8-9aa8-dc03541b4c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337430} {:answer/id #uuid "c4618295-ebc5-5ca8-a97f-d223c0994e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337778} {:answer/id #uuid "df52df73-6415-530f-99c1-35fac9f1a02b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "924d685b-06d1-54a6-83d2-4d89ca9bcafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337360} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7fe0bd-d0db-5b70-82fa-a698a8fe8d0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59fa73e3-7e11-58b8-85f9-3fcdbce086ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-71456" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-3y+z=0$ and $2x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,5,2⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-3,1⟩\\times ⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+2j+5k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,5⟩\\ne ⟨1,5,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes cannot be coincident.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}]}
